Enhancement: Read the log in the admin settings and through a JSON endpoint

Settings > General only offered the log level and a download of owncloud.log.
To see why a request failed, an admin had to download the whole file - on
installations with a large log that meant hundreds of megabytes - or ask
someone with shell access.

The log section now lists the newest entries below the download button: time,
level, app, message, user and request, newest first. The list can be filtered
by minimum level and app and searched for any text, including exception
classes, traces, request IDs, user names and addresses. "Load more" continues
with older entries. Clicking an entry (or pressing Enter on it) opens its
details: the full message, the exception with file and line, the trace and
the chain of exceptions that caused it, which Log::logException() writes as
separate "Caused by" lines and the view joins again. The download button stays.

The same data is available as JSON for admins at
GET /index.php/settings/admin/log/entries with the parameters offset, count
(1 to 200, default 50), level (minimum level 0 to 4), app and search. Besides
the browser session (with the request token, like every other settings
request) it accepts basic authentication with an admin's password or app
password, so scripts and monitoring tools can read the log without shell
access. The response contains next_offset, the byte position to pass as
offset for the next older page, and restarted when the log file was rotated in
the meantime.

The file is read backwards in blocks. A request reads at most 32 MB and returns
at most 200 entries or about 2 MB of text, so even a log file of several
gigabytes answers quickly; the view keeps reading on its own when a filter did
not match anything in that part. With a memory cache configured, an admin can
make 60 requests per minute (HTTP 429 with Retry-After after that). The viewer
needs log_type owncloud; with syslog or errorlog the endpoint answers 404 and
the section is not shown, as before.
